### What is var in lambda parameters?

Java 10 introduced `var` for local variables. Java 11 extends it to lambda parameters. This does not add type inference (lambdas already infer types) but enables annotations on parameters.

```java
// Without var: cannot annotate lambda params
(x, y) -> x + y

// With var: can annotate
(@NonNull var x, @NonNull var y) -> x + y
```

You must use var for ALL parameters or NONE. Mixing `var` and explicit types is not allowed.

### What are the new String methods?

```java
package com.bank.util;

public class Java11StringDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // isBlank(): true for empty or whitespace-only
        System.out.println("".isBlank());       // true
        System.out.println("   ".isBlank());    // true
        System.out.println("Rajesh".isBlank()); // false

        // strip(): Unicode-aware trim
        String padded = "  Rajesh  ";
        System.out.println(padded.strip());          // "Rajesh"
        System.out.println(padded.stripLeading());   // "Rajesh  "
        System.out.println(padded.stripTrailing());  // "  Rajesh"

        // repeat(n): duplicate string
        System.out.println("=-".repeat(20));  // =-=-=-...

        // lines(): split into Stream<String>
        String multiline = "Line1\nLine2\nLine3";
        multiline.lines().forEach(System.out::println);
    }
}
```

### How is strip() different from trim()?

| Method | Handles ASCII whitespace | Handles Unicode whitespace |
|--------|------------------------|---------------------------|
| `trim()` | Yes (chars <= 32) | No (misses `\u00A0` etc.) |
| `strip()` | Yes | Yes (uses Character.isWhitespace) |

```java
// Non-breaking space: trim misses it, strip catches it
String nbs = "\u00A0Rajesh\u00A0";
System.out.println(nbs.trim().length());   // 8 (still has \u00A0)
System.out.println(nbs.strip().length());  // 6 (\u00A0 removed)
```

### What is Predicate.not()?

```java
import java.util.function.Predicate;

// Before Java 11: lambda negation
lines.stream().filter(s -> !s.isBlank()).toList();

// Java 11: Predicate.not()
lines.stream().filter(Predicate.not(String::isBlank)).toList();
```

`Predicate.not()` is cleaner than `.negate()` which requires casting: `((Predicate<String>) String::isBlank).negate()`.

### What about Files.readString and writeString?

```java
import java.nio.file.*;

// One-liner file read
String content = Files.readString(Path.of("statement.txt"));

// One-liner file write
Files.writeString(Path.of("output.txt"), content);
```

These replace the verbose pattern of `new String(Files.readAllBytes(path))` and BufferedWriter boilerplate.

### What are common mistakes?

WRONG: Using `trim()` when Unicode whitespace is possible.

WRONG: Using `isEmpty()` when whitespace-only strings should be caught.

### What should a senior engineer know?

- Java 11 also removed Java EE modules (JAXB, JAX-WS) which is the main migration pain from Java 8.
- `Files.readString` loads the entire file into memory. For large files, use `Files.lines(path)` which is lazy and must be closed in a try-with-resources.
- `Collection.toArray(IntFunction)` was added: `list.toArray(String[]::new)` produces a typed array.
- The Epsilon GC (no-op collector) was added for performance testing and ultra-short-lived applications.
